    {
      "id": "panelbody-0476",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 12.0,
      "y": 3032.0,
      "width": 541.2,
      "height": 510.0,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "g-req-0473"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 532426364,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 345664353,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1755300000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "• One court with a FULL-GLASS back wall, and phone clamped 0–2.5 m behind it in a\n  CORNER of the glass rather than the centre, where the door frame, handle and\n  club logos sit. Full-glass back walls are a minority of club courts, so this is\n  the first question on the phone, not something discovered on a site visit.\n  Nothing may protrude into the playing surface — WSF requires a flush court.\n• The reflection fix is about $10: a rubber lens hood pressed flush against the\n  glass, plus killing or avoiding the gallery lights directly behind the phone.\n  Prove it on a dry run at a real court, not on pilot day.\n• Lighting: WSF rule 11's 300 lux (measured 1 m above the floor) is the\n  RECREATIONAL floor, not the competition bar. Club and county competition is ~500\n  lux, televised is 1,000+. Test against 500 and treat 300 as worst case.\n  Uniformity beats average — a court can average 400 lux with dark back corners,\n  which is exactly where the players stand; WSF also caps wall illumination\n  variation at 15% from average.\n• Mains flicker: 60 Hz in Canada and the US, 50 Hz in the UK. A mismatched shutter\n  bands the footage, and expo-camera exposes no manual shutter or ISO control.\n  Treat this as 'test it, and if it bands, that is the argument for\n  react-native-vision-camera' — not a week-one fix.\n• Calibration constants, all to be re-checked against the primary WSF PDF before\n  anything is hard-coded: court 9,750 × 6,400 mm; front-wall out line 4,570 mm;\n  back-wall out line 2,130 mm; front-wall service line 1,780 mm; short line 5,440\n  mm from the front wall (its far edge is the commonly quoted 4,260 mm from the\n  back wall); service boxes 1,600 × 1,600 mm. Two traps: the SIDE-wall out line\n  slopes 4,570 → 2,130, so any homography assuming a level side edge is wrong; and\n  the tin is 480 mm on a club court but 430 mm on the PSA men's tour, so it is a\n  parameter, not a constant.\n• Design for the real ball and the honest limits. The competition ball is BLACK\n  rubber, 40 mm — the contrast problem is black ball against black tin, dark floor\n  shadow and dark kit, never white-on-white — and club box-league players hit\n  roughly 100–160 km/h, not the ~270 km/h professional record. One back-wall\n  camera cannot adjudicate the tin, the out line, a serve fault against the\n  service line, or down / not-up (the double bounce), which is the single most\n  disputed call in club squash. Those need a ceiling or in-tin angle the club\n  would have to install.",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "• One court with a FULL-GLASS back wall, and phone clamped 0–2.5 m behind it in a\n  CORNER of the glass rather than the centre, where the door frame, handle and\n  club logos sit. Full-glass back walls are a minority of club courts, so this is\n  the first question on the phone, not something discovered on a site visit.\n  Nothing may protrude into the playing surface — WSF requires a flush court.\n• The reflection fix is about $10: a rubber lens hood pressed flush against the\n  glass, plus killing or avoiding the gallery lights directly behind the phone.\n  Prove it on a dry run at a real court, not on pilot day.\n• Lighting: WSF rule 11's 300 lux (measured 1 m above the floor) is the\n  RECREATIONAL floor, not the competition bar. Club and county competition is ~500\n  lux, televised is 1,000+. Test against 500 and treat 300 as worst case.\n  Uniformity beats average — a court can average 400 lux with dark back corners,\n  which is exactly where the players stand; WSF also caps wall illumination\n  variation at 15% from average.\n• Mains flicker: 60 Hz in Canada and the US, 50 Hz in the UK. A mismatched shutter\n  bands the footage, and expo-camera exposes no manual shutter or ISO control.\n  Treat this as 'test it, and if it bands, that is the argument for\n  react-native-vision-camera' — not a week-one fix.\n• Calibration constants, all to be re-checked against the primary WSF PDF before\n  anything is hard-coded: court 9,750 × 6,400 mm; front-wall out line 4,570 mm;\n  back-wall out line 2,130 mm; front-wall service line 1,780 mm; short line 5,440\n  mm from the front wall (its far edge is the commonly quoted 4,260 mm from the\n  back wall); service boxes 1,600 × 1,600 mm. Two traps: the SIDE-wall out line\n  slopes 4,570 → 2,130, so any homography assuming a level side edge is wrong; and\n  the tin is 480 mm on a club court but 430 mm on the PSA men's tour, so it is a\n  parameter, not a constant.\n• Design for the real ball and the honest limits. The competition ball is BLACK\n  rubber, 40 mm — the contrast problem is black ball against black tin, dark floor\n  shadow and dark kit, never white-on-white — and club box-league players hit\n  roughly 100–160 km/h, not the ~270 km/h professional record. One back-wall\n  camera cannot adjudicate the tin, the out line, a serve fault against the\n  service line, or down / not-up (the double bounce), which is the single most\n  disputed call in club squash. Those need a ceiling or in-tin angle the club\n  would have to install.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true
    },
