    },
    {
      "id": "panelbody-0480",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 595.25,
      "y": 3032.0,
      "width": 541.2,
      "height": 405.0,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "g-req-0477"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1292203170,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 625511560,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1755300000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "• Derive the file size from the codec the app actually sets, then quote one number\n  everywhere: HEVC at ~6 Mbps is roughly 2 GB per 45 minutes, H.264 at ~10 Mbps is\n  roughly 3.4 GB. The current documents disagree (3–4 GB vs 3–6 GB) and both\n  figures are guesses presented as constraints. Keep ~20 GB free on the device or\n  chunk-and-purge as segments upload.\n• Size the endurance target to squash, not to a demo: a best-of-5 PAR-11 club\n  match typically runs 30–50 minutes, so the real bar is one continuous 50-minute\n  take on a phone that is already throttling. A 20-minute match is a 3-0 blowout.\n• Chunking is the interruption story, and there is no expo-camera flag for it: iOS\n  suspends the AVCaptureSession when the app backgrounds, so an incoming call ends\n  the take. Five-minute chunks plus maxFileSize / maxDuration guards, plus 'enable\n  Do Not Disturb or Guided Access' on the laminated card at the court door.\n• No live streaming in v1. Record locally and upload opportunistically when the\n  phone rejoins club WiFi or the player's home network. One reliable upload point\n  in the building — front desk or a wired dock — with ~15 Mbps up, tested during\n  the site visit rather than during the pilot.\n• 'Squash courts are WiFi dead zones' is an inference from court construction\n  (interior or below-grade rooms, solid walls, glass), not evidence — no\n  squash-specific survey was found. Survey real clubs before it becomes an\n  architectural axiom.\n• Pin the data region to the pilot club's country on day one — an Ontario pilot\n  means Canadian region — and write down the retention window (30 days of raw\n  video by default) with every purge logged. Storage is linear in retained hours,\n  but per-match inference dominates cost. Also confirm which Azure credit vehicle\n  the $10k actually is and when it expires: $10k is not a standard Founders Hub\n  tier, and a new subscription has zero GPU quota by default, while the v0 audio\n  pipeline needs no GPU at all.",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "• Derive the file size from the codec the app actually sets, then quote one number\n  everywhere: HEVC at ~6 Mbps is roughly 2 GB per 45 minutes, H.264 at ~10 Mbps is\n  roughly 3.4 GB. The current documents disagree (3–4 GB vs 3–6 GB) and both\n  figures are guesses presented as constraints. Keep ~20 GB free on the device or\n  chunk-and-purge as segments upload.\n• Size the endurance target to squash, not to a demo: a best-of-5 PAR-11 club\n  match typically runs 30–50 minutes, so the real bar is one continuous 50-minute\n  take on a phone that is already throttling. A 20-minute match is a 3-0 blowout.\n• Chunking is the interruption story, and there is no expo-camera flag for it: iOS\n  suspends the AVCaptureSession when the app backgrounds, so an incoming call ends\n  the take. Five-minute chunks plus maxFileSize / maxDuration guards, plus 'enable\n  Do Not Disturb or Guided Access' on the laminated card at the court door.\n• No live streaming in v1. Record locally and upload opportunistically when the\n  phone rejoins club WiFi or the player's home network. One reliable upload point\n  in the building — front desk or a wired dock — with ~15 Mbps up, tested during\n  the site visit rather than during the pilot.\n• 'Squash courts are WiFi dead zones' is an inference from court construction\n  (interior or below-grade rooms, solid walls, glass), not evidence — no\n  squash-specific survey was found. Survey real clubs before it becomes an\n  architectural axiom.\n• Pin the data region to the pilot club's country on day one — an Ontario pilot\n  means Canadian region — and write down the retention window (30 days of raw\n  video by default) with every purge logged. Storage is linear in retained hours,\n  but per-match inference dominates cost. Also confirm which Azure credit vehicle\n  the $10k actually is and when it expires: $10k is not a standard Founders Hub\n  tier, and a new subscription has zero GPU quota by default, while the v0 audio\n  pipeline needs no GPU at all.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true
    },
