      "locked": false,
      "text": "Consent, safeguarding & minors",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Consent, safeguarding & minors",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true
    },
    {
      "id": "panelbody-0484",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1178.5,
      "y": 3032.0,
      "width": 541.2,
      "height": 555.0,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "g-req-0481"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1546143458,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 687639065,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1755300000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "• Pick ONE jurisdiction for the pilot. Milton, Ontario means Canada is the P0\n  legal surface; England Squash / CPSU and US Squash move to market-entry.\n  Correction to the previous draft: PIPEDA is NOT stricter than UK/EU GDPR — GDPR\n  has explicit-consent standards, mandatory DPIAs, hard-deadline data-subject\n  rights and fines to 4% of global turnover against PIPEDA's CAD $100k ceiling.\n  Build to the UK GDPR engineering bar and you clear PIPEDA; the reverse is not\n  true. The strictest Canadian bar is Quebec's Law 25, not PIPEDA.\n• Assume a DPIA is required and ship a template. Session-based, consent-triggered\n  recording does NOT keep this out of DPIA territory — under UK GDPR a DPIA is\n  triggered by children's data, by biometric processing and by innovative\n  technology, independently of whether capture is continuous. Filming minors plus\n  pose estimation almost certainly requires one. (The previous draft claimed both\n  that a DPIA was avoided and that purges were logged 'for the DPIA'.)\n• AUDIO consent is a separate and larger ask than video, and the entire v0 signal\n  is audio. On-court conversation between two players is private speech in both\n  Canada and the UK. app.json currently describes the microphone as being used 'so\n  playback includes the sound of the rally' — that under-describes it, and\n  shipping that audio to Azure for ML is a purpose change requiring fresh consent.\n  Fix the string now, and make audio its own checkbox on the consent gate.\n• Under-16s: parent or carer consent BEFORE filming, linked to the child's account\n  and revocable at any time, with a non-consenting player hard-blocking the\n  session. Four things the earlier draft missed — what happens to junior footage\n  when the child turns 16 or leaves the club; background checks (Vulnerable Sector\n  Check in Ontario, DBS in the UK) for anyone with access to junior footage,\n  including Karan; a hard rule that no junior footage ever appears in an outbound\n  or social artefact; and the fact that junior box leagues are where clubs film\n  most, so this is the highest-volume use case, not an edge case.\n• A coach voice-over sent to a junior is a 1:1 asynchronous adult-child channel.\n  Most NGB safeguarding codes require it to be visible to a second adult or routed\n  through a parent. Write that rule before the feature is built, not after a\n  safeguarding officer finds it.\n• The consent gate covers 'every player' and nothing else — not the gallery, not\n  the pair warming up, not faces reflected in the glass back wall. Alongside it: a\n  written photography, filming AND livestreaming policy template shipped with the\n  app; signage at the court entrance naming the operator, the purpose and a\n  data-rights contact; and a per-session audit log, which is exactly what the\n  OPC's 'documented record of every decision to record' expectation asks for.",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "• Pick ONE jurisdiction for the pilot. Milton, Ontario means Canada is the P0\n  legal surface; England Squash / CPSU and US Squash move to market-entry.\n  Correction to the previous draft: PIPEDA is NOT stricter than UK/EU GDPR — GDPR\n  has explicit-consent standards, mandatory DPIAs, hard-deadline data-subject\n  rights and fines to 4% of global turnover against PIPEDA's CAD $100k ceiling.\n  Build to the UK GDPR engineering bar and you clear PIPEDA; the reverse is not\n  true. The strictest Canadian bar is Quebec's Law 25, not PIPEDA.\n• Assume a DPIA is required and ship a template. Session-based, consent-triggered\n  recording does NOT keep this out of DPIA territory — under UK GDPR a DPIA is\n  triggered by children's data, by biometric processing and by innovative\n  technology, independently of whether capture is continuous. Filming minors plus\n  pose estimation almost certainly requires one. (The previous draft claimed both\n  that a DPIA was avoided and that purges were logged 'for the DPIA'.)\n• AUDIO consent is a separate and larger ask than video, and the entire v0 signal\n  is audio. On-court conversation between two players is private speech in both\n  Canada and the UK. app.json currently describes the microphone as being used 'so\n  playback includes the sound of the rally' — that under-describes it, and\n  shipping that audio to Azure for ML is a purpose change requiring fresh consent.\n  Fix the string now, and make audio its own checkbox on the consent gate.\n• Under-16s: parent or carer consent BEFORE filming, linked to the child's account\n  and revocable at any time, with a non-consenting player hard-blocking the\n  session. Four things the earlier draft missed — what happens to junior footage\n  when the child turns 16 or leaves the club; background checks (Vulnerable Sector\n  Check in Ontario, DBS in the UK) for anyone with access to junior footage,\n  including Karan; a hard rule that no junior footage ever appears in an outbound\n  or social artefact; and the fact that junior box leagues are where clubs film\n  most, so this is the highest-volume use case, not an edge case.\n• A coach voice-over sent to a junior is a 1:1 asynchronous adult-child channel.\n  Most NGB safeguarding codes require it to be visible to a second adult or routed\n  through a parent. Write that rule before the feature is built, not after a\n  safeguarding officer finds it.\n• The consent gate covers 'every player' and nothing else — not the gallery, not\n  the pair warming up, not faces reflected in the glass back wall. Alongside it: a\n  written photography, filming AND livestreaming policy template shipped with the\n  app; signage at the court entrance naming the operator, the purpose and a\n  data-rights contact; and a per-session audit log, which is exactly what the\n  OPC's 'documented record of every decision to record' expectation asks for.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true
    },
