    {
      "id": "panelbody-0488",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1761.75,
      "y": 3032.0,
      "width": 541.2,
      "height": 390.0,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "g-req-0485"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1416019863,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 336726271,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1755300000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "• Write a default operating rule for the PILOT, separate from the eventual\n  contract: the club is controller, RacquetAI is processor, each player may obtain\n  a personal copy, and nothing is published without club sign-off. 'No precedent\n  found, this needs a lawyer' is honest, but the first match is filmed in six days\n  and a pilot cannot run with the question open.\n• Fix the direct contradiction before the notice is printed: the pilot notice says\n  'footage stays with the club' while the same week's plan hand-carries that\n  footage off the device to Azure. Either the notice authorises processing by\n  RacquetAI — named, with the region — or the Azure run does not happen.\n• Deletion is a shared-subject problem, because every squash match has exactly two\n  players. Written answers needed: who may request deletion (either player, the\n  account holder, only the club?); what happens to the OTHER player's copy of the\n  same file; and what happens to a coach's voice-over recorded on top of it.\n• Some exports are unrecoverable: a clip AirDropped into someone's Photos library\n  cannot be deleted by us. Either the deletion path ships first, or export is\n  restricted to Files-only with no Photos write until it exists. Do not build an\n  un-deletable escape hatch before the consent and erasure mechanism exists.\n• Publish a deletion SLA: a stated turnaround, a logged confirmation to the\n  requester, and propagation to derived clips, thumbnails, rally flags, share\n  links and any Azure copy — plus an explicit statement of what survives\n  (aggregate stats with no footage) and why.\n• The authorization model is the second question a GM asks and the documents never\n  answer it: who at the club may watch whose match — the front desk, every member,\n  or only the two players and the pro? Add a DPA with the club, a named\n  sub-processor list (Azure, with its region), and a breach process naming who\n  tells the club and the affected members if footage leaks.",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "• Write a default operating rule for the PILOT, separate from the eventual\n  contract: the club is controller, RacquetAI is processor, each player may obtain\n  a personal copy, and nothing is published without club sign-off. 'No precedent\n  found, this needs a lawyer' is honest, but the first match is filmed in six days\n  and a pilot cannot run with the question open.\n• Fix the direct contradiction before the notice is printed: the pilot notice says\n  'footage stays with the club' while the same week's plan hand-carries that\n  footage off the device to Azure. Either the notice authorises processing by\n  RacquetAI — named, with the region — or the Azure run does not happen.\n• Deletion is a shared-subject problem, because every squash match has exactly two\n  players. Written answers needed: who may request deletion (either player, the\n  account holder, only the club?); what happens to the OTHER player's copy of the\n  same file; and what happens to a coach's voice-over recorded on top of it.\n• Some exports are unrecoverable: a clip AirDropped into someone's Photos library\n  cannot be deleted by us. Either the deletion path ships first, or export is\n  restricted to Files-only with no Photos write until it exists. Do not build an\n  un-deletable escape hatch before the consent and erasure mechanism exists.\n• Publish a deletion SLA: a stated turnaround, a logged confirmation to the\n  requester, and propagation to derived clips, thumbnails, rally flags, share\n  links and any Azure copy — plus an explicit statement of what survives\n  (aggregate stats with no footage) and why.\n• The authorization model is the second question a GM asks and the documents never\n  answer it: who at the club may watch whose match — the front desk, every member,\n  or only the two players and the pro? Add a DPA with the club, a named\n  sub-processor list (Azure, with its region), and a breach process naming who\n  tells the club and the affected members if footage leaks.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true
    },
