      "locked": false,
      "text": "• Clubs already spend roughly $100–550/mo on booking software (CourtReserve\n  $199–549/mo, Skedda $99–199/mo). RacquetAI is an add-on inside that envelope,\n  not a replacement. Fix the currency drift too — quote the pilot's country (CAD\n  for Ontario) and convert everything else rather than mixing $, £ and € in one\n  argument.\n• The anchor price in an operator's head is CourtReserve's own $25/mo Video\n  Recording Integration add-on. Anything above roughly $50/court/mo needs a\n  court-hour argument to survive the first pricing conversation.\n• Per-player willingness to pay is proven in squash: Club Locker's Canadian\n  consumer tier is $6.67/mo billed annually or $11.99/mo month-to-month, and its\n  PAID tier already promises 'match media'. Sit inside that band — and note this\n  makes Club Locker a plausible competitor on video, not only a distribution rail.\n• Model unit economics as cost-per-match, not cost-per-GB, because per-match\n  inference dominates storage. Rally Vision's reported $10 basic / $50 premium per\n  match is the direct evidence for that, but it is a marketing page read once and\n  independently unverified — treat it as an anchor to test, not a fact to quote.\n• Fixed installs are the ceiling and the reason almost no club has anything:\n  InteractiveSQUASH is quoted 'starting at €16,550' plus €1,000–5,000 of operator\n  prerequisite works, installed only via ASB SquashCourts — and that figure sits\n  on a page whose surrounding content dates to around 2017, so it is stale rather\n  than current pricing. Any RacquetAI hardware SKU must land well under\n  €2,000/court; realistically it is a $200 clamp and a retired iPhone.\n• Pilot terms: one club, one or two courts, free through the LA28 / Rally28 growth\n  window in exchange for named-reference rights, utilisation data, and the\n  footage-ownership rule signed as written. Nothing about money is signed this\n  week.",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "• Clubs already spend roughly $100–550/mo on booking software (CourtReserve\n  $199–549/mo, Skedda $99–199/mo). RacquetAI is an add-on inside that envelope,\n  not a replacement. Fix the currency drift too — quote the pilot's country (CAD\n  for Ontario) and convert everything else rather than mixing $, £ and € in one\n  argument.\n• The anchor price in an operator's head is CourtReserve's own $25/mo Video\n  Recording Integration add-on. Anything above roughly $50/court/mo needs a\n  court-hour argument to survive the first pricing conversation.\n• Per-player willingness to pay is proven in squash: Club Locker's Canadian\n  consumer tier is $6.67/mo billed annually or $11.99/mo month-to-month, and its\n  PAID tier already promises 'match media'. Sit inside that band — and note this\n  makes Club Locker a plausible competitor on video, not only a distribution rail.\n• Model unit economics as cost-per-match, not cost-per-GB, because per-match\n  inference dominates storage. Rally Vision's reported $10 basic / $50 premium per\n  match is the direct evidence for that, but it is a marketing page read once and\n  independently unverified — treat it as an anchor to test, not a fact to quote.\n• Fixed installs are the ceiling and the reason almost no club has anything:\n  InteractiveSQUASH is quoted 'starting at €16,550' plus €1,000–5,000 of operator\n  prerequisite works, installed only via ASB SquashCourts — and that figure sits\n  on a page whose surrounding content dates to around 2017, so it is stale rather\n  than current pricing. Any RacquetAI hardware SKU must land well under\n  €2,000/court; realistically it is a $200 clamp and a retired iPhone.\n• Pilot terms: one club, one or two courts, free through the LA28 / Rally28 growth\n  window in exchange for named-reference rights, utilisation data, and the\n  footage-ownership rule signed as written. Nothing about money is signed this\n  week.",
